# Test helpers leak temp dirs: b017, b019 and defines suites never clean up

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found 2026-08-30 while gating B039, whose own fixture leak was fixed there.

## Defect

Several test helpers build a temp dir keyed on `std::process::id()` and call
`remove_dir_all` only at the START of the run, never at the end. Each run uses
a fresh PID, so every invocation leaves its dirs behind forever.

Measured on this machine after B039's own leak was fixed: **24 stray
`/tmp/oplsp-*` dirs**, some over nine hours old —

    13  oplsp-b017-*
     6  oplsp-b019-*
     4  oplsp-defines-*
     1  oplsp-ucbad-*

and **0** `oplsp-b039-*`, which is the point: B039 fixed its own and the
others were left.

## Why it is worth a ticket

Small in bytes (~100 MB accumulated) but this batch lost real time to /tmp
exhaustion twice, and a full /tmp does not fail cleanly here — it produced 44
spurious `StorageFull` test failures in one lane and silently LOST another
lane's command output. A suite that litters on every invocation contributes to
a failure mode that costs far more than the disk does.

## Work

Copy the fix B039 already landed: a `Fixture` struct with a `Drop` impl that
removes its base dir, so cleanup happens on the unwind path too — a test that
panics is exactly when the dir gets left behind today.

Find the helpers by grepping `temp_dir()` in `tests/`. Do not just delete the
accumulated dirs; that is the symptom.

## Test plan

Verify by MEASUREMENT, not inspection, the way B039 did: count
`/tmp/oplsp-*` before and after two consecutive full runs and assert it does
not grow. A code review of a `Drop` impl cannot tell you it actually fires on
the paths that matter.

Variants:
- a test that PANICS still cleans up (the unwind path is the whole point)
- two concurrent runs do not delete each other's dirs
- `just test-hermetic`, which runs binaries directly under an isolated env,
  also cleans up

## Related

B039 (fixed the same defect in `tests/execute_command_tests.rs`; copy that
`Drop` guard), B045 (hermeticity gate — these helpers run under it too).

## Lane decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

**Serialised with B066 into one lane (`lane-b065-b066`), B065 FIRST.**
File overlap: both work in `tests/` (`config_reload_tests.rs`,
`resolved_defines_tests.rs`, `cli_check_tests.rs`, `hermeticity_canary.rs`),
and B066 adds tests under a synthetic HOME that want exactly the non-leaking
`Fixture`/`Drop` helper B065 builds. Landing B065 first shrinks B066.

Measure, do not inspect. The ticket's own test plan is the gate: count
`/tmp/oplsp-*` before and after two consecutive full runs. A `Drop` impl that
reads correctly and never fires is the failure mode here, so a review of the
code is not evidence — only the count is.

## CORRECTION — the premise above is half wrong (lane-b065-b066, verified by
## coordinator, 2026-08-31)

Measured, not inspected. **The named `oplsp-*` helpers are NOT the leak**, and
the gate this ticket specified would have passed while leaking 640 dirs.

**Two leak classes, one defect:**

- **Class A — unconditional, ~320 dirs per green run.** Helpers with no
  end-of-test cleanup at all: `openplanet-lsp-typedb-*` (~150),
  `openplanet-lsp-gitignore-*` (27), `opl-config-tui-*` (24),
  `openplanet-lsp-ws-*` (22), `opl-user-config-*` (10), `opl-config-cmd-*` (8),
  `openplanet-lsp-p*-*` (16), `ols-*` (10), misc (~53). This is all the volume.
- **Class B — unwind path only, 0 per green run.** The `oplsp-b017/b019/
  defines/ucbad` helpers this ticket named. They DO remove at end of test
  (`src/config.rs:4130` opens, callers close at `:4382/:4412/:4510/:4548`;
  97 `remove_dir_all` calls vs 14 helper opens). They leak only when a test
  panics, fails, or is killed — which is why the 24 stray dirs observed are
  the residue of red or interrupted runs, matching this batch's two /tmp
  exhaustion incidents.

Coordinator verified both halves by reading the code independently.

**The 141,080 accumulated dirs need no separate ticket.** 320/run x ~440 runs
~= 141k, and only ~960 of /tmp's ~142k entries are not this repo's prefixes.
It is entirely this defect, via the helpers the ticket failed to name.

**The specified gate was vacuous.** "Count `/tmp/oplsp-*` across two runs and
assert it does not grow" reads 24 -> 24 -> 24 on green runs while 640 new dirs
land under other prefixes. It measures the one family that does not leak on the
happy path. Worse, a raw `/tmp` diff cannot attribute anything at all here:
sibling lanes run the same suite into the same `/tmp` (observed 142,041 ->
143,835 with no run by the measuring lane in between).

**Replacement gate, load-bearing:** run with
`TMPDIR=<isolated dir>` (`std::env::temp_dir()` honours it; verified no test
hardcodes a `/tmp` path it creates — the `"/tmp..."` literals in `src/` are all
path-parsing assertions). The isolated count starts at 0 and must return to 0.
Report `/tmp/oplsp-*` too, but label it as the weak signal it is.

**Class B is invisible to any count on a green run.** The deliberate-panic test
is not a variant on top of a moving count — it is the *only* instrument that
can observe Class B at all.

**Decision (coordinator):** single `TempDir` guard with a `Drop` impl (B039's
shape) hoisted to one place and applied to both classes, ~105 `temp_dir()` call
sites across `src/` and `tests/`. Exposed as `#[doc(hidden)] pub mod testing;`
on the lib rather than duplicated into `tests/common/mod.rs` — integration
tests are separate crates so `#[cfg(test)]` items are invisible to them, and
duplicating the one component whose entire job is unwind-path correctness is
how a drifted copy silently stops cleaning.